criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
`dist-workspace.toml` declares:

- `cargo-dist-version = "0.33.0"`;
- the six targets unchanged (`aarch64`/`x86_64` `apple-darwin`, `pc-windows-msvc`, `unknown-linux-musl`), installers `shell` and `powershell`, `checksum = "sha256"`, `install-updater = false`, `hosting = "github"`;
- `install-path = ["$RAYX_HOME/bin", "~/.rayx/bin"]`;
- `github-attestations = true`;
- `[dist.github-custom-runners]` building `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` on `windows-2022` with host `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` (no cargo-xwin container);
- `pr-run-mode = "upload"`;
- no `allow-dirty`.

`.github/workflows/release.yml` is the output of `dist generate` for that config: `dist plan` succeeds without reporting the CI out of date, the tag trigger still matches `v<semver>` tags, and the Windows ARM64 matrix entry has no container.

Verified by the new group `tests/release_config.rs` and by `dist plan`.

`release-plz.toml` at the repository root configures the single package `rayx-cli` for a git-only, unpublished release:

- `git_only = true`, `git_tag_enable = true`, tag name `v{{ version }}`, `git_release_enable = false` (dist creates the GitHub Release), `changelog_update = true`, `semver_check = false` (the library target is not a public API), `publish = false`;
- a `[changelog]` (git-cliff) configuration that groups Conventional Commit types into sections, drops `chore(agentx)`/`chore(release)` noise, and writes headings in the `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD` form that dist's changelog parser reads;
- the tag form agrees with what the other consumers build: dist's tag trigger and the `v{version}` path in `src/setup/wsl.rs` (asserted in the test, not just by eye).

Verified by the new group `tests/release_automation.rs`.

`.github/workflows/release-plz.yml` runs on pushes to `main` (and `workflow_dispatch`) in `rayx-ui/RayX.CLI` only (`if: github.repository == 'rayx-ui/RayX.CLI'`):

- a `release` job runs `release-plz release` and a `release-pr` job runs `release-plz release-pr` (with a concurrency group that does not cancel a running release);
- both check out with full history (`fetch-depth: 0`) and `token: ${{ secrets.RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN }}`, and pass the same secret as `GITHUB_TOKEN` to the release-plz action, so the release PR triggers `ci.yml` and the pushed tag triggers `release.yml`;
- `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` is the only secret referenced; actions are pinned to a major version or commit; the workflow installs the toolchain the action needs and nothing else.

`ci.yml` is unchanged (pull requests only).

Verified by the new group `tests/release_automation.rs` (YAML parsed or asserted structurally) and by GitHub accepting the workflow (no "workflow file issue" run).

`docs/releasing.md` explains for the owner:

- how a release happens (commit with Conventional Commits, review and merge the release PR, the tag starts the Release workflow), which commit types bump which version part, and that `feat!`/`BREAKING CHANGE` means a major release and needs a paired RayX change when it touches the app grammar;
- how to create `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` (fine-grained, resource owner `rayx-ui`, only `RayX.CLI`, Contents and Pull requests read/write, expiry), that release PRs and tags then appear as the owner, and what silently stops when it expires and how to rotate it;
- the by-hand fallback (bump, changelog, `git tag vX.Y.Z`, push the tag);
- how to check a release (`gh release view`, `gh attestation verify <archive> --repo rayx-ui/RayX.CLI`).

The README links it. Verified by review.

The new group `tests/dependency_sources.rs` replaces the registry-only assertions of `tests/package_layout.rs` (which keeps its other checks). For `Cargo.toml` and `cargo metadata` it:

- accepts crates.io dependencies and `git` dependencies with an `https://` URL pinned by `rev`;
- rejects `path` dependencies, `git` dependencies selected by `branch`, `tag` or nothing, and any dependency on the private `rayx-ui/RayX` repository (exactly that repository, not `RayX.CLI` or other public siblings);
- accepts a `[patch.<registry>]` entry only when it follows the same source rules and the manifest line above it is a comment containing an upstream issue or PR URL; still rejects `[replace]`.

The rules are a function the test exercises on inline sample manifests (each accept and reject case above) and then on the real manifest.

In `src/setup/wsl.rs`:

- the Linux binary is `$HOME/.rayx/bin/rayx`; the inner run is that path with `setup <same flags>`;
- when that binary does not report `rayx <running version>`, the distribution runs (through `--exec bash -lc`, as every WSL script) the release's versioned installer `https://github.com/rayx-ui/RayX.CLI/releases/download/v<version>/rayx-cli-installer.sh` piped to `sh`, non-interactively; it no longer extracts the archive by hand into `~/.local/bin`;
- a matching installed version is not reinstalled; an architecture without a Linux release still fails before any download with the existing actionable message;
- `RAYX_WSL_BINARY` still copies a local build, now to `$HOME/.rayx/bin/rayx` (a development override without a receipt);
- `--check` (print mode) runs no download or install.

Verified by the new group `tests/wsl_install.rs` (scripted runner, as in `tests/wsl_plan.rs`), with `tests/wsl_plan.rs` updated where it asserted the old path or archive URL.

The README install section has:

- the latest one-liners for macOS/Linux and Windows (URLs unchanged) and the same with a pinned version (`releases/download/v1.0.0/...`);
- where `rayx` goes (`~/.rayx/bin`, `$RAYX_HOME/bin` when set), that the installer adds it to PATH (new shell needed), and that running the installer again updates in place, as does `rayx self update`;
- how to check an archive's provenance with `gh attestation verify`;
- a note for archives downloaded by hand in a browser on macOS (`xattr -d com.apple.quarantine rayx`) and that the installers need no such step;
- a link to `docs/releasing.md`;
- the "until the first release" and `--branch feature/initial-setup` instructions removed (building from source stays under its own heading).

`tests/package_layout.rs` README assertions still pass. Verified by review.
